00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:26,560
using this kind of printing-log approach to look at things,

198
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:27,840
because it's a bit more efficient that way

199
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:31,840
Now, as for how to get this

200
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:33,920
serial port device

201
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:35,100
put it to use

202
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:36,720
and what exactly UART is

203
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:37,920
these are the concepts we can

204
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:39,340
briefly introduce to you here

205
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:43,060
so this UART

206
00:06:43,060 --> 00:06:45,120
the full name is called

207
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:47,000
it's actually an English acronym

208
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:51,340
namely Universal Asynchronous Receiver and Transmitter

209
00:06:51,340 --> 00:06:53,680
so it actually expresses a few concepts

210
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:54,620
that is, it is a

211
00:06:54,620 --> 00:06:56,900
Universal means unified

212
00:06:56,900 --> 00:06:58,940
that is, historically speaking,

213
00:06:58,940 --> 00:07:00,420
it took quite a lot of things

214
00:07:00,420 --> 00:07:01,820
and made them fairly general-purpose

215
00:07:01,820 --> 00:07:02,420
and unified

216
00:07:02,420 --> 00:07:03,700
and then it embodies a

217
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:06,180
that is, it has the concept of asynchronous

218
00:07:06,180 --> 00:07:08,100
Asynchronous, the concept of asynchronous

219
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:10,440
and then Receiver and Transmitter

220
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:11,780
which is what's written down below here

221
00:07:11,780 --> 00:07:14,020
It can send and receive at the same time

222
00:07:14,020 --> 00:07:15,180
That diagram we just saw

223
00:07:15,180 --> 00:07:15,660
Everyone has seen it

224
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:16,800
It has two wires for sending and receiving

225
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:17,040
Right?

226
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:18,540
It sends and receives simultaneously

227
00:07:18,540 --> 00:07:20,660
So we often

228
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:21,920
just call it the serial cable

229
00:07:21,920 --> 00:07:22,820
Now, the serial cable

230
00:07:22,820 --> 00:07:24,100
mainly because it has one characteristic

231
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:25,240
which is the concept of serial

232
00:07:25,240 --> 00:07:26,860
So what do we mean by serial?

233
00:07:26,860 --> 00:07:27,340
That is to say,

234
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:29,040
because when it sends and receives,

235
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:29,800
there are only two wires,

236
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:30,220
right?

237
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:31,360
Think about it. If we

238
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:33,160
take one of the wires

239
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:34,000
as an example,

240
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:34,840
then what it means is

241
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:35,960
We only have one channel

242
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:37,140
that can send and receive data.

243
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:38,800
Now, when this data is sent and received,

244
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:39,400
at that time,

245
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:39,880
it actually,

246
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:41,060
what we call serial,

247
00:07:41,060 --> 00:07:41,780
means that,

248
00:07:41,780 --> 00:07:42,780
its,

249
00:07:42,780 --> 00:07:46,260
the data inside it,

250
00:07:46,260 --> 00:07:47,580
is sent out bit by bit,

251
00:07:47,580 --> 00:07:49,040
one bit at a time.

252
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:50,940
Now, more specifically,

253
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:52,180
that is to say, for example, let's say,

254
00:07:52,180 --> 00:07:55,200
we want to send a character A.

255
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:56,880
We know that this character A,

256
00:07:56,880 --> 00:07:58,960
when represented in our computer,

257
00:07:58,960 --> 00:07:59,720
it's ASCII, right?

258
00:07:59,720 --> 00:08:00,560
It's actually,

259
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:02,920
an 8-bit piece of data, isn't it?

260
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:04,480
As for what exactly it is,

261
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:05,280
I can't remember exactly,

262
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:06,540
I think it was something like sixty-something, or whatever it was.

263
00:08:06,540 --> 00:08:08,320
So actually it's an 8-bit piece of data.

264
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:09,600
Now, an 8-bit piece of data,

265
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:10,840
as represented in our computer,

266
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:11,840
is simply 8 bits.

267
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:13,160
8 bits,

268
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:14,980
a sequence of 0s and 1s in those bit positions.

269
00:08:14,980 --> 00:08:16,660
I'll just make up the actual values here,

270
00:08:16,660 --> 00:08:18,140
12345678.

271
00:08:18,140 --> 00:08:19,020
Say we have a piece of data like this.

272
00:08:19,020 --> 00:08:20,800
Now for this piece of data,

273
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:23,060
how does it actually get sent out over the serial port?

274
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:25,260
Well, this is serial transmission.

275
00:08:25,260 --> 00:08:26,400
It's sent one bit at a time.

276
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:28,300
In other words, if it's 8 bits,

277
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:30,120
then you have to send 8 electrical pulses,

278
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:32,660
meaning the signal level has to change 8 times.

279
00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:34,860
A 0 represents a low level,

280
00:08:34,860 --> 00:08:36,340
and a 1 represents a high level.

