how do we know it's about to start sending data?

561
00:16:20,640 --> 00:16:21,480
So it

562
00:16:21,480 --> 00:16:22,600
the transmitting end

563
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:25,640
will actively pull this high level down

564
00:16:25,640 --> 00:16:26,880
pull down for one bit

565
00:16:26,880 --> 00:16:28,660
So how long is this one bit?

566
00:16:28,660 --> 00:16:29,800
Actually, you can work it out.

567
00:16:29,800 --> 00:16:31,820
Because, as we said, once the baud rate is set

568
00:16:31,820 --> 00:16:32,700
you know that this

569
00:16:32,700 --> 00:16:34,480
duration of one bit

570
00:16:34,480 --> 00:16:36,020
is actually, for example, 115200

571
00:16:36,020 --> 00:16:37,500
so the duration of this one bit is

572
00:16:37,500 --> 00:16:39,780
1/115200 of a second

573
00:16:39,780 --> 00:16:40,340
Think about it, isn't that right?

574
00:16:40,340 --> 00:16:42,560
So how long does this one bit last?

575
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:43,700
Because the horizontal axis is time,

576
00:16:43,700 --> 00:16:45,300
so how long does this one bit last?

577
00:16:45,300 --> 00:16:47,460
These can all be measured.

578
00:16:47,460 --> 00:16:49,520
So at the very beginning,

579
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:50,480
it sits at a high level, which means

580
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:51,880
it is in the idle state

581
00:16:51,880 --> 00:16:53,900
So once we're ready to start sending data

582
00:16:53,900 --> 00:16:55,340
how do I tell the other side

583
00:16:55,340 --> 00:16:56,420
that I'm about to start working?

584
00:16:56,420 --> 00:16:58,340
Well, it pulls the line low

585
00:16:58,340 --> 00:16:59,360
that is, it takes the voltage

586
00:16:59,360 --> 00:17:00,700
from high to low

587
00:17:00,700 --> 00:17:02,000
and holds it for one bit

588
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:03,100
of time,

589
00:17:03,100 --> 00:17:05,860
and that signals the start.

590
00:17:05,860 --> 00:17:07,260
That is the start bit.

591
00:17:07,260 --> 00:17:08,320
It means exactly this:

592
00:17:08,320 --> 00:17:10,920
the sender first sends a low level, a zero,

593
00:17:10,920 --> 00:17:12,560
to indicate the beginning of the character transmission,

594
00:17:12,560 --> 00:17:13,800
right?

595
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:15,980
Then, for the actual character being transmitted,

596
00:17:15,980 --> 00:17:17,300
as we know, generally,

597
00:17:17,300 --> 00:17:18,760
we just talk about one byte,

598
00:17:18,760 --> 00:17:20,220
one piece of ASCII data,

599
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:21,860
that's what we call the transmitted data.

600
00:17:21,860 --> 00:17:24,300
Now, this gray part at the end,

601
00:17:24,300 --> 00:17:26,880
that means it's sending this data

602
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:29,280
Now, this data,

603
00:17:29,280 --> 00:17:30,780
according to the rules of the protocol,

604
00:17:30,780 --> 00:17:32,060
has what's called a data length

605
00:17:32,060 --> 00:17:33,140
This data length

606
00:17:33,140 --> 00:17:34,960
can be 5, 6, 7, or 8 bits

607
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:35,720
But in general,

608
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:36,760
we usually just use one form,

609
00:17:36,760 --> 00:17:39,440
with the length set to 8

610
00:17:39,440 --> 00:17:40,820
bits

611
00:17:40,820 --> 00:17:42,440
The ASCII standard uses 8 bits for everything

612
00:17:42,440 --> 00:17:44,420
So when we send an 'A',

613
00:17:44,420 --> 00:17:45,360
it takes up 8 bits

614
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:46,820
In other words, this entire

615
00:17:46,820 --> 00:17:49,200
whole process

616
00:17:49,200 --> 00:17:50,680
can actually only send one byte

617
00:17:50,680 --> 00:17:51,400
Does everyone understand?

618
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:52,480
Every time it sends a byte,

619
00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:53,160
there has to be a

620
00:17:53,160 --> 00:17:54,880
process like this

621
00:17:54,880 --> 00:17:58,660
So, we send 8 bits

622
00:17:58,660 --> 00:17:59,600
Once that's sent,

623
00:17:59,600 --> 00:18:00,500
it forms one 8-bit unit.

624
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:01,180
Then,

625
00:18:01,180 --> 00:18:02,600
the rule is to send the lowest bit first,

626
00:18:02,600 --> 00:18:03,620
and the highest bit last.

627
00:18:03,620 --> 00:18:04,860
The example I gave you earlier,

628
00:18:04,860 --> 00:18:06,260
I was actually explaining this very concept,

629
00:18:06,260 --> 00:18:07,660
that is, with an 8-bit piece of data,

630
00:18:07,660 --> 00:18:08,620
each of its bits —

631
00:18:08,620 --> 00:18:09,700
do we send the low bit first,

632
00:18:09,700 --> 00:18:10,600
or the high bit first?

633
00:18:10,600 --> 00:18:11,440
This is also something

634
00:18:11,440 --> 00:18:13,300
that has to be specified in the protocol.

635
00:18:13,300 --> 00:18:14,660
And then,

636
00:18:14,660 --> 00:18:15,920
after the data has been sent,

637
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:16,820
it will be followed by

638
00:18:16,820 --> 00:18:19,060
one additional bit.

639
00:18:19,060 --> 00:18:19,740
This bit,

640
00:18:19,740 --> 00:18:20,700
we call it

641
00:18:20,700 --> 00:18:22,300
machine parity bit

642
00:18:22,300 --> 00:18:23,680
It's called parity in English.

643
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:26,140
Now, this parity bit,

644
00:18:26,140 --> 00:18:27,640
what it really does,

645
00:18:27,640 --> 00:18:29,460
its actual purpose is to,

646
00:18:29,460 --> 00:18:31,800
the original idea behind its design,

647
00:18:31,800 --> 00:18:32,500
is essentially this:

648
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:34,880
it helps us check

649
00:18:34,880 --> 00:18:36,420
whether the data got corrupted

650
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:38,180
during transmission.

651
00:18:38,180 --> 00:18:39,740
For example, as we all know,

652
00:18:39,740 --> 00:18:41,460
circuits can be subject to interference.

653
00:18:41,460 --> 00:18:42,620
Once electromagnetic waves get disturbed,

654
00:18:42,620 --> 00:18:43,900
a one you sent from your end,

655
00:18:43,900 --> 00:18:46,000
might be read as a zero on the other side.

656
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:47,560
You see what I mean, right?

657
00:18:47,560 --> 00:18:48,860
There can be some instability,

658
00:18:48,860 --> 00:18:49,740
causing some interference.

659
00:18:49,740 --> 00:18:51,680
So how do we detect

660
00:18:51,680 --> 00:18:53,580
that the data has been disturbed?

661
00:18:53,580 --> 00:18:54,420
A problem occurs.

662
00:18:54,420 --> 00:18:55,640
So we introduced a

663
00:18:55,640 --> 00:18:56,920
concept of parity bits.

664
00:18:56,920 --> 00:18:58,560
That is to say, this parity check

665
00:18:58,560 --> 00:18:59,000
well,

666
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:00,520
in practice there are a few kinds.

667
00:19:00,520 --> 00:19:01,520
One is no parity check,

668
00:19:01,520 --> 00:19:01,880
that is to say,

669
00:19:01,880 --> 00:19:04,100
for instance, in general,

670
00:19:04,100 --> 00:19:04,980
we might assume that this,

671
00:19:04,980 --> 00:19:06,380
normally, say, when you

672
00:19:06,380 --> 00:19:07,980
connect your board to yourself,

673
00:19:07,980 --> 00:19:08,840
just hooked up like this,

674
00:19:08,840 --> 00:19:10,040
if there is no strong

675
00:19:10,040 --> 00:19:11,160
electromagnetic interference in the environment,

676
00:19:11,160 --> 00:19:12,800
the data generally won't go wrong; it's

677
00:19:12,800 --> 00:19:13,780
still fairly stable.

678
00:19:13,780 --> 00:19:15,320
So if we save one bit,

679
00:19:15,320 --> 00:19:16,800
the transmission speed will definitely be a bit faster.

680
00:19:16,800 --> 00:19:18,040
And this one

681
00:19:18,040 --> 00:19:19,480
You can also configure it to

682
00:19:19,480 --> 00:19:20,240
that is, not to do parity checking.

683
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:21,260
Usually we seem to configure it this way,

684
00:19:21,260 --> 00:19:22,040
without parity checking.

685
00:19:22,040 --> 00:19:23,860
But you can also configure it to do parity checking.

686
00:19:23,860 --> 00:19:24,300
Now, for parity checking,

687
00:19:24,300 --> 00:19:25,640
there are two types: odd parity and even parity.

688
00:19:25,640 --> 00:19:27,300
So what is odd parity?

689
00:19:27,300 --> 00:19:27,520
Well,

690
00:19:27,520 --> 00:19:30,040
that is, if among the data bits,

691
00:19:30,040 --> 00:19:31,180
the number of ones is even,

692
00:19:31,180 --> 00:19:31,900
then the parity bit is 1.

693
00:19:31,900 --> 00:19:32,420
What does that mean?

694
00:19:32,420 --> 00:19:33,580
It means that as the sender,

695
00:19:33,580 --> 00:19:35,500
say you want to send out a piece of data,

696
00:19:35,500 --> 00:19:36,360
and in the data you are sending,

697
00:19:36,360 --> 00:19:37,200
within these 8 bits,

698
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:40,340
say there are 7 ones,

699
00:19:40,340 --> 00:19:41,460
then that means

700
00:19:41,460 --> 00:19:42,460
there is an odd number of one bits.

701
00:19:42,460 --> 00:19:42,700
So

702
00:19:42,700 --> 00:19:46,120
if the number of ones is odd,

703
00:19:46,120 --> 00:19:47,860
this part here is written wrong,

704
00:19:47,860 --> 00:19:48,100
sorry about that.

705
00:19:48,100 --> 00:19:50,000
This should probably be the odd

706
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:50,640
it should be odd parity,

707
00:19:50,640 --> 00:19:51,800
down here it says even,

708
00:19:51,800 --> 00:19:52,780
up here it should be odd.

709
00:19:52,780 --> 00:19:54,400
That is to say, if the sender

710
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:55,680
sends this data,

711
00:19:55,680 --> 00:19:57,280
and there are 7 bits

712
00:19:57,280 --> 00:19:59,040
that are 1 in it,

713
00:19:59,040 --> 00:19:59,600
then that means

714
00:19:59,600 --> 00:20:02,000
the number of ones is odd,

715
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:03,540
so the sender, on this

716
00:20:03,540 --> 00:20:05,000
parity bit position here,

717
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:06,100
sets it to 1,

718
00:20:06,100 --> 00:20:07,320
to tell the other side that

719
00:20:07,320 --> 00:20:08,940
in the data I'm sending,

720
00:20:08,940 --> 00:20:09,540
there are actually

721
00:20:09,540 --> 00:20:11,360
then the number of 1s

722
00:20:11,360 --> 00:20:12,100
is actually odd

723
00:20:12,100 --> 00:20:13,780
So after the receiver gets it,

724
00:20:13,780 --> 00:20:14,560
based on this bit,

725
00:20:14,560 --> 00:20:15,820
it checks whether your odd parity data

726
00:20:15,820 --> 00:20:17,000
matches what it says

727
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:17,540
the expressed meaning

728
00:20:17,540 --> 00:20:17,980
is the same or not,

729
00:20:17,980 --> 00:20:18,920
doing a simple check.

730
00:20:18,920 --> 00:20:19,540
Everyone understands, right?

731
00:20:19,540 --> 00:20:21,180
This simple parity check,

732
00:20:21,180 --> 00:20:22,140
it's just a simple check.

733
00:20:22,140 --> 00:20:24,140
Actually, it's not completely accurate,

734
00:20:24,140 --> 00:20:25,360
but after all, it serves

735
00:20:25,360 --> 00:20:26,560
a certain checking purpose.

736
00:20:26,560 --> 00:20:30,620
Now, this even parity,

737
00:20:30,620 --> 00:20:31,460
is just the opposite.

738
00:20:31,460 --> 00:20:32,880
And so on and so forth.

739
00:20:32,880 --> 00:20:33,840
What else is there?

740
00:20:33,840 --> 00:20:34,660
The parity bit is always 1.

741
00:20:34,660 --> 00:20:35,640
Why is the parity bit always 0?

742
00:20:35,640 --> 00:20:36,980
Actually, it also has a special purpose.

743
00:20:36,980 --> 00:20:38,480
I won't go into the details here.

744
00:20:38,480 --> 00:20:40,160
So what this means is,

745
00:20:40,160 --> 00:20:41,620
after it finishes sending the data,

746
00:20:41,620 --> 00:20:43,160
it usually adds a parity bit as well.

747
00:20:43,160 --> 00:20:44,320
Or there might not be this bit at all.

748
00:20:44,320 --> 00:20:45,920
So after sending is done,

749
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:47,160
how does it indicate that

750
00:20:47,160 --> 00:20:48,820
one byte has been completely sent?

751
00:20:48,820 --> 00:20:50,180
Because we have a beginning,

752
00:20:50,180 --> 00:20:51,080
we also need an end.

753
00:20:51,080 --> 00:20:52,560
This is the concept of synchronization.

754
00:20:52,560 --> 00:20:53,800
That is to say, before I send,

755
00:20:53,800 --> 00:20:54,620
I wave my hand,

756
00:20:54,620 --> 00:20:55,620
and after I finish sending,

757
00:20:55,620 --> 00:20:56,440
I also wave my hand,

758
00:20:56,440 --> 00:20:57,820
telling the other side that it's done.

759
00:20:57,820 --> 00:20:58,080
Right?

760
00:20:58,080 --> 00:20:59,540
So for the start,

761
00:20:59,540 --> 00:21:00,660
we signal the other side with a low level

762
00:21:00,660 --> 00:21:02,040
a single-bit low level

763
00:21:02,040 --> 00:21:03,000
to tell the other side.

764
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:03,940
So how do we

765
00:21:03,940 --> 00:21:06,680
tell the other side that it's finished?

766
00:21:06,680 --> 00:21:08,580
It's actually on this line,

767
00:21:08,580 --> 00:21:09,760
we need to actually produce

768
00:21:09,760 --> 00:21:11,100
a high level of one bit, one and a half bits,

769
00:21:11,100 --> 00:21:12,840
or two bits

770
00:21:12,840 --> 00:21:14,060
and this is configurable as well.

771
00:21:14,060 --> 00:21:15,280
So in this way,

772
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:16,220
we tell the other side

773
00:21:16,220 --> 00:21:19,040
that one byte has been sent,

774
00:21:19,040 --> 00:21:19,960
that one byte has been sent.

775
00:21:19,960 --> 00:21:21,980
So our entire...,

776
00:21:21,980 --> 00:21:22,560
that is to say,

777
00:21:22,560 --> 00:21:23,860
once you say you want to send

778
00:21:23,860 --> 00:21:25,500
eight bytes,

779
00:21:25,500 --> 00:21:27,500
or say ten bytes,

780
00:21:27,500 --> 00:21:28,400
then for each byte

781
00:21:28,400 --> 00:21:29,520
they all have to go through such a process.

782
00:21:29,520 --> 00:21:30,600
So this is one

783
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:31,460
plus that makes one frame.

784
00:21:31,460 --> 00:21:32,240
This frame,

785
00:21:32,240 --> 00:21:33,680
a frame in UART,

786
00:21:33,680 --> 00:21:34,720
its this

787
00:21:34,720 --> 00:21:35,760
actually has a start

788
00:21:35,760 --> 00:21:37,260
and also adds a parity bit,

789
00:21:37,260 --> 00:21:38,860
and together these make up

790
00:21:38,860 --> 00:21:39,300
this one frame.

791
00:21:39,300 --> 00:21:39,980
Does everyone understand?

792
00:21:39,980 --> 00:21:41,200
So this sending process

793
00:21:41,200 --> 00:21:42,600
is actually not as

794
00:21:42,600 --> 00:21:43,120
simple as you might imagine.

795
00:21:43,120 --> 00:21:44,440
It's not just that you send data,

796
00:21:44,440 --> 00:21:45,120
pure data.

797
00:21:45,120 --> 00:21:45,960
It actually has some

798
00:21:45,960 --> 00:21:48,080
bytes with special meanings in it,

799
00:21:48,080 --> 00:21:50,220
which serve some synchronization purpose,

800
00:21:50,220 --> 00:21:51,520
and a verification purpose.

801
00:21:51,520 --> 00:21:52,660
You get what I mean, right?

802
00:21:52,660 --> 00:21:55,260
This is the communication protocol of the serial port.

803
00:21:55,260 --> 00:21:56,660
Then the transceivers on both sides

804
00:21:56,660 --> 00:21:57,980
have to follow this protocol,

805
00:21:57,980 --> 00:22:00,640
doing some checks on the data on the line,

806
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:02,140
so that the data is

807
00:22:02,140 --> 00:22:04,420
correctly sent and received.

808
00:22:04,420 --> 00:22:07,060
Now, as for this information,

809
00:22:07,060 --> 00:22:10,460
when we use this serial port transceiver,

810
00:22:10,460 --> 00:22:11,920
what it actually means is that,

811
00:22:11,920 --> 00:22:13,680
for us as software engineers,

812
00:22:13,680 --> 00:22:14,460
we need to configure it.

813
00:22:14,460 --> 00:22:15,020
You understand that, right?

814
00:22:15,020 --> 00:22:16,200
You see, as I just said,

815
00:22:16,200 --> 00:22:18,100
the parity bit can be there or not.

816
00:22:18,100 --> 00:22:18,740
So whether you want it or not,

817
00:22:18,740 --> 00:22:19,820
that's up to you to decide.

818
00:22:19,820 --> 00:22:21,360
Then, on the peripheral interface,

819
00:22:21,360 --> 00:22:22,840
there are some special registers,

820
00:22:22,840 --> 00:22:24,440
that let us configure it.

821
00:22:24,440 --> 00:22:26,200
So, before we correctly

822
00:22:26,200 --> 00:22:27,600
send out a piece of data,

823
00:22:27,600 --> 00:22:29,060
first there is a certain initialization process.

824
00:22:29,060 --> 00:22:30,200
That is, we need to configure some devices.

825
00:22:30,200 --> 00:22:31,660
All of our hardware devices,

826
00:22:31,660 --> 00:22:32,300
including writing drivers,

827
00:22:32,300 --> 00:22:33,280
actually follow this same idea.

828
00:22:33,280 --> 00:22:35,480
Before using them, you have to do some configuration,

829
00:22:35,480 --> 00:22:36,240
do some initialization.

830
00:22:36,240 --> 00:22:37,740
Now, why do we need to do initialization?

831
00:22:37,740 --> 00:22:39,220
There is definitely a reason behind it.

832
00:22:39,220 --> 00:22:40,000
So we look at this interface,

833
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:41,320
why is it done this way?

834
00:22:41,320 --> 00:22:43,720
I've given you some introduction here.

835
00:22:43,720 --> 00:22:46,520
This is the concept of the communication protocol.

836
00:22:46,520 --> 00:22:50,340
So next,

837
00:22:50,340 --> 00:22:51,280
let's take a look at

838
00:22:51,280 --> 00:22:53,540
when we actually do the programming,

839
00:22:53,540 --> 00:22:56,040
how exactly we operate this thing.

840
00:22:56,040 --> 00:22:57,920
Now, as we mentioned earlier,

841
00:22:57,920 --> 00:22:59,280
So this, we

842
00:22:59,280 --> 00:23:03,340
this NS16550A,

843
00:23:03,340 --> 00:23:03,960
actually it is

844
00:23:03,960 --> 00:23:06,340
the emulator we are using now,

845
00:23:06,340 --> 00:23:07,960
the one it emulates,

846
00:23:07,960 --> 00:23:09,900
this serial transceiver,

