So what is the polling method?

1676
00:50:44,640 --> 00:50:45,560
It actually means this.

1677
00:50:45,560 --> 00:50:48,480
Suppose we want to send a piece of data.

1678
00:50:48,480 --> 00:50:50,860
Inside this UART,

1679
00:50:50,860 --> 00:50:51,880
there is actually a register.

1680
00:50:51,880 --> 00:50:52,880
This register...

1681
00:50:52,880 --> 00:50:53,600
Inside it,

1682
00:50:53,600 --> 00:50:54,920
if you want to put data in,

1683
00:50:54,920 --> 00:50:56,040
this register

1684
00:50:56,040 --> 00:50:58,740
is connected to its data lines and sends it out.

1685
00:50:58,740 --> 00:51:01,300
So the content in this register,

1686
00:51:01,300 --> 00:51:01,620
as we know,

1687
00:51:01,620 --> 00:51:03,320
you put one byte in,

1688
00:51:03,320 --> 00:51:04,900
it puts one byte in at a time,

1689
00:51:04,900 --> 00:51:05,800
you put one byte in,

1690
00:51:05,800 --> 00:51:06,880
and it sends one out.

1691
00:51:06,880 --> 00:51:07,800
Put one in, send one out.

1692
00:51:07,800 --> 00:51:09,040
But this sending, as we know,

1693
00:51:09,040 --> 00:51:09,940
takes time.

1694
00:51:09,940 --> 00:51:10,400
Right, everyone?

1695
00:51:10,400 --> 00:51:12,000
In other words, this...

1696
00:51:12,000 --> 00:51:13,940
you can't just send into it whenever you like,

1697
00:51:13,940 --> 00:51:16,860
because if that byte hasn't finished sending yet

1698
00:51:16,860 --> 00:51:18,420
and you put a second byte in,

1699
00:51:18,420 --> 00:51:21,380
you'll overwrite the previous byte,

1700
00:51:21,380 --> 00:51:22,880
and your data will get corrupted.

1701
00:51:22,880 --> 00:51:25,040
So, to achieve this effect,

1702
00:51:25,040 --> 00:51:26,660
there is a way, that is to say,

1703
00:51:26,660 --> 00:51:29,860
we have the polling method or the interrupt method

1704
00:51:29,860 --> 00:51:31,560
to know when to put the data in,

1705
00:51:31,560 --> 00:51:33,120
so that it can be sent out correctly.

1706
00:51:33,120 --> 00:51:35,460
Now, what we call polling,

1707
00:51:35,460 --> 00:51:38,040
means we can keep checking a register,

1708
00:51:38,040 --> 00:51:40,620
to see whether this register is currently free.

1709
00:51:40,620 --> 00:51:42,040
If it is free,

1710
00:51:42,040 --> 00:51:43,040
we send the data out,

1711
00:51:43,040 --> 00:51:44,120
right?

1712
00:51:44,120 --> 00:51:47,020
If the register is not free,

1713
00:51:47,020 --> 00:51:47,760
then we don't send it.

1714
00:51:47,760 --> 00:51:48,760
That's what polling is.

1715
00:51:48,760 --> 00:51:50,640
It is implemented through a loop,

1716
00:51:50,640 --> 00:51:52,920
constantly checking it.

1717
00:51:52,920 --> 00:51:54,920
Then there is also the interrupt method,

1718
00:51:54,920 --> 00:51:56,580
which means that when this register

1719
00:51:56,580 --> 00:51:57,620
is free,

1720
00:51:57,620 --> 00:52:01,040
we let this UART device...

1721
00:52:01,040 --> 00:52:02,860
It will notify us through an interrupt.

1722
00:52:02,860 --> 00:52:04,560
It's like giving us a call to say,

1723
00:52:04,560 --> 00:52:06,440
"I'm free now,

1724
00:52:06,440 --> 00:52:07,860
you can put it in."

1725
00:52:07,860 --> 00:52:08,840
At that point,

1726
00:52:08,840 --> 00:52:12,220
we can use the interrupt approach.

1727
00:52:12,220 --> 00:52:13,320
As for the interrupt,

1728
00:52:13,320 --> 00:52:14,520
since we haven't covered that concept yet,

1729
00:52:14,520 --> 00:52:15,520
we'll hold off on it for now.

1730
00:52:15,520 --> 00:52:18,680
Let's focus on how this polling works.

1731
00:52:18,680 --> 00:52:20,180
Here,

1732
00:52:20,180 --> 00:52:22,100
there are two registers involved.

1733
00:52:22,100 --> 00:52:27,520
One is called the transmit holder,

1734
00:52:27,520 --> 00:52:31,000
that is, the THR register.

1735
00:52:31,000 --> 00:52:31,900
This register,

1736
00:52:31,900 --> 00:52:34,240
as we call it, is the register for putting data.

1737
00:52:34,240 --> 00:52:36,120
The data we want to send,

1738
00:52:36,120 --> 00:52:38,240
we just need to put it into this register,

1739
00:52:38,240 --> 00:52:39,660
and it will send it out.

1740
00:52:39,660 --> 00:52:42,340
So how do we know this register

1741
00:52:42,340 --> 00:52:44,620
already finished being processed by it,

1742
00:52:44,620 --> 00:52:45,800
already sent out.

1743
00:52:45,800 --> 00:52:47,920
Then we use a register on this side

1744
00:52:47,920 --> 00:52:50,320
called the line status register.

1745
00:52:50,320 --> 00:52:52,060
There's a special bit in it that tells us

1746
00:52:52,060 --> 00:52:55,420
whether the transmit register is already free.

1747
00:52:55,420 --> 00:52:57,560
So combining these two registers,

1748
00:52:57,560 --> 00:53:00,380
we can implement data transmission.

1749
00:53:00,380 --> 00:53:04,640
Let's look at how the code is actually written.

1750
00:53:04,640 --> 00:53:06,620
The code is actually quite simple.

1751
00:53:06,620 --> 00:53:09,060
You can see that we've implemented a

1752
00:53:09,060 --> 00:53:10,920
function called uartputc,

1753
00:53:10,920 --> 00:53:11,660
this function.

1754
00:53:11,660 --> 00:53:12,880
This function actually mimics

1755
00:53:12,880 --> 00:53:14,440
the putc function in our C library.

1756
00:53:14,440 --> 00:53:15,480
As you know, putc

1757
00:53:15,480 --> 00:53:17,180
only sends one byte at a time,

1758
00:53:17,180 --> 00:53:17,800
right?

1759
00:53:17,800 --> 00:53:20,380
And here you can see

1760
00:53:20,380 --> 00:53:21,580
there's just a while loop here,

1761
00:53:21,580 --> 00:53:23,720
What is this while loop doing?

1762
00:53:23,720 --> 00:53:25,520
What this while loop is actually doing is

1763
00:53:25,520 --> 00:53:27,060
As you can see, what it's really doing is

1764
00:53:27,060 --> 00:53:28,980
continuously reading a register

1765
00:53:28,980 --> 00:53:31,420
specifically this lsr register

1766
00:53:31,420 --> 00:53:33,040
reading out the value inside this register

1767
00:53:33,040 --> 00:53:35,060
and at the same time, through this AND operation,

1768
00:53:35,060 --> 00:53:37,800
checking whether bit 5 of this register

1769
00:53:37,800 --> 00:53:39,900
is currently equal to 1

1770
00:53:39,900 --> 00:53:41,840
If it is 1,

1771
00:53:41,840 --> 00:53:43,620
if at this point, that is,

1772
00:53:43,620 --> 00:53:44,700
if it equals 0,

1773
00:53:44,700 --> 00:53:48,340
then the value read out here

1774
00:53:48,340 --> 00:53:49,760
is not yet 1

1775
00:53:49,760 --> 00:53:51,080
and if it's not 1, that means

1776
00:53:51,080 --> 00:53:53,140
this register is currently not free

1777
00:53:53,140 --> 00:53:56,340
that is, our THR register here

1778
00:53:56,340 --> 00:53:57,560
is currently not free yet

1779
00:53:57,560 --> 00:53:59,380
and while it's not free, it just spins in this infinite loop

1780
00:53:59,380 --> 00:54:02,020
So when does this code...

1781
00:54:02,020 --> 00:54:03,560
If we can get down to this part here,

1782
00:54:03,560 --> 00:54:05,740
that means the LSR we just read out,

1783
00:54:05,740 --> 00:54:07,300
bit 5 of it, is 1.

1784
00:54:07,300 --> 00:54:07,780
Think about it, right?

1785
00:54:07,780 --> 00:54:10,540
So when it's 1, it means this register,

1786
00:54:10,540 --> 00:54:12,900
the THR here, is free.

1787
00:54:12,900 --> 00:54:14,240
Being free, it goes down to the next step,

1788
00:54:14,240 --> 00:54:15,980
and at this point, through a write operation,

1789
00:54:15,980 --> 00:54:18,420
we write the character into this register.

1790
00:54:18,420 --> 00:54:20,380
So you can actually think of this line as meaning,

1791
00:54:20,380 --> 00:54:22,960
the data has been sent out to the window,

1792
00:54:22,960 --> 00:54:27,400
thus achieving synchronization with this sending device.

1793
00:54:27,400 --> 00:54:29,460
this synchronization effect

1794
00:54:29,460 --> 00:54:32,740
We call this approach the polling method.

1795
00:54:32,740 --> 00:54:34,080
The polling method.

1796
00:54:34,080 --> 00:54:38,910
So after implementing the sending of a single character,

1797
00:54:38,910 --> 00:54:39,990
well, you know, when sending,

1798
00:54:39,990 --> 00:54:42,030
say I want to send a hello or something,

1799
00:54:42,030 --> 00:54:44,310
what we actually do is break it down into each character,

1800
00:54:44,310 --> 00:54:46,850
and call putc once for every character.

1801
00:54:46,850 --> 00:54:48,210
and just send it out

1802
00:54:48,210 --> 00:54:49,330
See what I mean?

1803
00:54:49,330 --> 00:54:52,910
And that's how the character gets transmitted.

1804
00:54:52,910 --> 00:54:53,850
So using the serial port,

1805
00:54:53,850 --> 00:54:54,850
we can achieve this effect.

1806
00:54:54,850 --> 00:54:56,850
As for the related code,

1807
00:54:56,850 --> 00:54:59,070
you can go take a look.

1808
00:54:59,070 --> 00:55:10,110
It's the example we just talked about,

1809
00:55:10,110 --> 00:55:16,760
in that code's os,

1810
00:55:16,760 --> 00:55:21,060
the example inside hello rbos.

1811
00:55:21,060 --> 00:55:21,960
Feel free to check it out.

1812
00:55:21,960 --> 00:55:23,440
We'll leave this one to you,

1813
00:55:23,440 --> 00:55:25,300
and in the second class later,

1814
00:55:25,300 --> 00:55:27,020
we'll spend some time going through it together.

1815
00:55:27,020 --> 00:55:28,440
You can try this example out as an exercise,

1816
00:55:28,440 --> 00:55:29,840
and see what we just covered,

1817
00:55:29,840 --> 00:55:30,740
combining it with what we just talked about.

1818
00:55:30,740 --> 00:55:31,320
Go and take a look.

1819
00:55:31,320 --> 00:55:35,280
Alright, let's, let's continue.

1820
00:55:35,280 --> 00:55:48,450
Now that I've gone over the code with you,

1821
00:55:48,450 --> 00:55:49,890
I forgot to give you a quick demo

1822
00:55:49,890 --> 00:56:00,230
So let's take a look at that simple Hello demo from just now

1823
00:56:00,230 --> 00:56:03,230
After we compile it

1824
00:56:03,230 --> 00:56:05,190
once we just run make run,

1825
00:56:05,190 --> 00:56:06,410
we can see

1826
00:56:06,410 --> 00:56:10,530
that a Hello appears on the screen

1827
00:56:10,530 --> 00:56:11,950
RBOS

1828
00:56:11,950 --> 00:56:14,370
Now, when it prints this out here,

1829
00:56:14,370 --> 00:56:15,750
what we need to understand is that

1830
00:56:15,750 --> 00:56:21,230
we are actually controlling the UART device inside the emulator

1831
00:56:21,230 --> 00:56:25,110
and you can imagine that there is a virtual UART line

1832
00:56:25,110 --> 00:56:27,950
and through that UART line from the device,

1833
00:56:27,950 --> 00:56:30,930
it gets printed to our host machine's console

1834
00:56:30,930 --> 00:56:33,550
and then these characters appear on the console

1835
00:56:33,550 --> 00:56:35,470
and those characters are what we printed out

1836
00:56:35,470 --> 00:56:36,890
And the corresponding code

1837
00:56:36,890 --> 00:56:39,170
is in our

1838
00:56:39,170 --> 00:56:42,250
hello,

1839
00:56:42,250 --> 00:56:45,850
our kono.c code,

1840
00:56:45,850 --> 00:56:51,230
this function that we talked about earlier

1841
00:56:51,230 --> 00:56:53,810
In other words, it's actually jumped into from assembly

1842
00:56:53,810 --> 00:56:54,990
the first C function, right?

1843
00:56:54,990 --> 00:56:56,370
Now, in this C function,

1844
00:56:56,370 --> 00:56:58,030
right at the start, it runs

1845
00:56:58,030 --> 00:57:00,130
a UART initialization,

1846
00:57:00,130 --> 00:57:02,710
getting that serial port device initialized.

1847
00:57:02,710 --> 00:57:04,630
Then, through a puts,

1848
00:57:04,630 --> 00:57:05,710
this puts,

1849
00:57:05,710 --> 00:57:06,670
inside it actually,

1850
00:57:06,670 --> 00:57:08,610
for each of its characters,

1851
00:57:08,610 --> 00:57:09,850
calls that putc,

1852
00:57:09,850 --> 00:57:11,730
and then takes each character

1853
00:57:11,730 --> 00:57:12,590
and, through the serial port,

1854
00:57:12,590 --> 00:57:14,430
prints it out to our console.

1855
00:57:14,430 --> 00:57:16,050
And after the printing is done,

1856
00:57:16,050 --> 00:57:18,290
of course we just write an infinite loop,

1857
00:57:18,290 --> 00:57:19,070
and it just stops there.

1858
00:57:19,070 --> 00:57:20,090
So as we can see,

1859
00:57:20,090 --> 00:57:22,050
in our output here,

1860
00:57:22,050 --> 00:57:24,110
it doesn't go any further at this point.

1861
00:57:24,110 --> 00:57:25,890
So that's basically the effect.

1862
00:57:25,890 --> 00:57:27,150
Let me briefly explain it to you.

1863
